From 71ccb17887536e6ed87e80f59494a4b8bcd7f0b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andree Toonk <atoonk@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 23:11:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] device: expose transport keypairs for an inline fast path

A forwarding plane that owns its NIC receives a transport message in a DMA frame and
would like to decrypt it there, on the same goroutine, and transmit from there. Routing
every packet through this package's pipeline instead costs a copy into a pooled buffer,
three goroutine hand-offs and two channel operations, and above ~1.3 Mpps the single
RoutineReadFromTUN goroutine is the ceiling however many receive queues feed it.

This exposes the minimum such a caller needs: the negotiated transport state as
constructed AEADs and indices, never key bytes; a replay check safe against this
package's own receive path; a nonce reservation that cannot collide with this package's
own sends; the per-batch liveness calls that keep rekeying and timers working; and a
keypair-lifecycle observer so the caller can keep a receiver-index table. The handshake,
timers, cookies, rate limiting, roaming and configuration all stay here.

The replay window takes a mutex, unconditionally. Upstream keeps the window safe by
validating each keypair from one goroutine; once a keypair can be reached from outside
this package that is no longer enough, and two unsynchronised updates to one ring block
lose each other's bit, which makes an already-seen counter look new. ReserveNonces is a
compare-and-swap that never advances past RejectAfterMessages, because an unconditional
add would leave only 2^13 counts before the counter wrapped and low nonces were reused
under the same key.

PeerPublicKey deliberately takes no lock. The observer runs with the peer's keypairs
lock held and, from BeginSymmetricSession, its handshake mutex, so any method that takes
either would deadlock the device if the callback called it; remoteStatic is written once
in NewPeer and never again, so this one is safe and the contract says which are not.

Ten tests, five of them adversarial: nonce uniqueness with compare-and-swap reservers
racing this package's own adders near the limit, the replay window accepting each
counter exactly once under eight validators, which slot an established keypair lands in
per role, an expired keypair refusing both gates of the sender, and which locks are held
when the observer runs. Each fails when the guard it targets is removed.
